// 2012 GS1 (Prelims) Set A — Transcribed from "E:\UPSC question paper\2012\2012 prilims question& ans.docx"
// Contains question, choices (A, B, C, D), answer, explanation, and upsc review points
export const paper = { year: 2012, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"In the areas covered under the Panchayat (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996, what is the role/power of Gram Sabha?\n1. Gram Sabha has the power to prevent alienation of land in the Scheduled Areas.\n2. Gram Sabha has ownership of minor forest produce.\n3. Recommendation of Gram Sabha is required for granting prospecting licence or mining lease for any mineral in the Scheduled Areas.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["PESA","Gram Sabha","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"PESA strengthens the role of Gram Sabha in Scheduled Areas.\nGram Sabha has powers relating to prevention of land alienation.\nIt has ownership rights over minor forest produce.\nThe source does not treat the third statement as a correct power.","revisionPoints":"PESA → 1996.\nApplies to Scheduled Areas under the Fifth Schedule.\nGram Sabha has an important role in protecting tribal customs, resources and land rights.\nMinor forest produce is an important community resource."},
{"no":2,"text":"In the Parliament of India, the purpose of an adjournment motion is:","A":"To allow a discussion on a definite matter of urgent public importance","B":"To let opposition members collect information from the ministers","C":"To allow a reduction of specific amount in demand for grant","D":"To postpone proceedings because of inappropriate behaviour","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Adjournment Motion","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"An adjournment motion is used to draw attention to a specific and urgent matter of public importance.\nIt interrupts the normal business of the House.\nIt is an important parliamentary mechanism for holding the government accountable.\nIt differs from a cut motion, which relates to demands for grants.","revisionPoints":"Adjournment Motion → definite matter of urgent public importance.\nIt involves interruption of normal parliamentary business.\nIt is primarily associated with the Lok Sabha.\nIt is an important instrument of parliamentary control over the executive."},
{"no":3,"text":"How does the National Biodiversity Authority (NBA) help protect Indian agriculture?\n1. NBA checks biopiracy and protects indigenous and traditional genetic resources.\n2. NBA directly monitors and supervises scientific research on genetic modification of crop plants.\n3. Application for Intellectual Property Rights related to genetic/biological resources cannot be made without approval of NBA.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["National Biodiversity Authority","Biopiracy","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"NBA works under the framework of the Biological Diversity Act.\nIt helps regulate access to biological resources and associated traditional knowledge.\nIt helps prevent biopiracy and protects India's biological resources.\nThe source identifies statements 1 and 3 as correct.","revisionPoints":"NBA → National Biodiversity Authority.\nConcerned with conservation and sustainable use of biological diversity.\nHelps regulate access to biological resources.\nImportant concept → biopiracy and benefit sharing."},
{"no":4,"text":"The National Green Tribunal Act, 2010 was enacted in consonance with which constitutional provision?\n1. Right to a healthy environment as part of Article 21.\n2. Grants for raising the level of administration in Scheduled Areas under Article 275(1).\n3. Powers and functions of Gram Sabha under Article 243A.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["National Green Tribunal Act","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The right to a healthy environment has been interpreted as part of the Right to Life under Article 21.\nThe National Green Tribunal was established to provide specialised adjudication for environmental disputes.\nArticles 275(1) and 243A concern different constitutional subjects.\nTherefore, only statement 1 is relevant.","revisionPoints":"NGT Act → 2010.\nNGT deals with environmental protection and related disputes.\nArticle 21 has been judicially expanded to include environmental rights.\nNGT follows principles such as sustainable development and precautionary principle."},
{"no":5,"text":"If the National Water Mission is properly and completely implemented, how will it impact the country?\n1. Part of urban water needs will be met through recycling wastewater.\n2. Coastal cities may use ocean water through appropriate technologies.\n3. All Himalayan rivers will be linked to Peninsular rivers.\n4. Farmers' bore-well and pump-set expenses will be completely reimbursed by the Government.","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["National Water Mission","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Water recycling can reduce pressure on freshwater resources in urban areas.\nTechnologies such as desalination can provide water to water-stressed coastal cities.\nRiver linking is not the objective stated in the National Water Mission in this manner.\nComplete reimbursement of farmers' groundwater extraction expenses is also not its stated objective.","revisionPoints":"National Water Mission → part of India's climate-change action framework.\nFocuses on water conservation and efficient water use.\nImportant approaches include recycling, reuse and demand management.\nWater-use efficiency is central to sustainable water management."},
{"no":6,"text":"Which of the following are Gandhian Principles reflected in the Directive Principles of State Policy?\n1. Securing a Uniform Civil Code.\n2. Organising village Panchayats.\n3. Promoting cottage industries in rural areas.\n4. Securing reasonable leisure and cultural opportunities for workers.","A":"1, 2 and 4 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Directive Principles of State Policy","Gandhian Principles","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Organising village Panchayats reflects Gandhian decentralisation.\nPromotion of cottage industries reflects Gandhi's emphasis on village-based economic activity.\nUniform Civil Code belongs to another category of DPSP.\nReasonable leisure and cultural opportunities are not classified as Gandhian principles.","revisionPoints":"DPSPs are commonly grouped into:\nGandhian principles\nSocialist principles\nLiberal-intellectual principles\nArticle 40 → organisation of village Panchayats.\nArticle 43 → promotion of cottage industries.\nGandhian DPSPs emphasise rural development and decentralisation."},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Union Territories are not represented in the Rajya Sabha.\n2. The Chief Election Commissioner adjudicates election disputes.\n3. Parliament consists only of Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Union Territories","Rajya Sabha","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Some Union Territories are represented in the Rajya Sabha.\nElection disputes are generally adjudicated through the judicial process, not by the Chief Election Commissioner.\nUnder Article 79, Parliament consists of the President, Rajya Sabha and Lok Sabha.\nHence none of the three statements is correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 79 → Parliament = President + Rajya Sabha + Lok Sabha.\nCertain Union Territories have representation in Rajya Sabha.\nElection Commission conducts elections but does not function as a general court for election disputes.\nElection disputes are governed by constitutional and statutory mechanisms."},
{"no":8,"text":"With reference to consumers' rights in India, which statements are correct?\n1. Consumers are empowered to take samples for food testing.\n2. No fee is required when a consumer files a complaint in any consumer forum.\n3. In case of death of a consumer, the legal heir can file a complaint on their behalf.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["Consumer Protection","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Consumers have rights and mechanisms for testing food products.\nConsumer complaints may involve prescribed fees depending on the applicable framework, so statement 2 is incorrect.\nA legal heir can pursue a consumer complaint in the event of the consumer's death.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Consumer protection aims to safeguard consumers against defective goods and deficient services.\nImportant consumer rights include safety, information, choice and redressal.\nLegal heirs can pursue eligible consumer claims.\nConsumer dispute redressal mechanisms provide institutional remedies."},
{"no":9,"text":"Regarding the office of the Lok Sabha Speaker, consider the following statements:\n1. The Speaker holds office during the pleasure of the President.\n2. The Speaker need not be a member of the House at election but must become a member within six months.\n3. The Speaker's resignation is addressed to the Deputy Speaker.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Lok Sabha Speaker","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Speaker is elected by the Lok Sabha and does not hold office at the pleasure of the President.\nThe Speaker must be a member of the Lok Sabha; there is no six-month provision of the type stated.\nThe Speaker resigns by writing to the Deputy Speaker.\nHence only statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Speaker → presiding officer of Lok Sabha.\nElected by the Lok Sabha from among its members.\nResignation → addressed to the Deputy Speaker.\nSpeaker's removal requires a resolution of the Lok Sabha with the required constitutional procedure."},
{"no":10,"text":"Which of the following are included in the original jurisdiction of the Supreme Court?\n1. Dispute between Government of India and one or more States.\n2. Dispute regarding elections to Parliament or State Legislature.\n3. Dispute between Government of India and a Union Territory.\n4. Dispute between two or more States.","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 4","D":"3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Original Jurisdiction","Supreme Court","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in certain federal disputes.\nThis includes disputes between the Union and one or more States.\nIt also includes disputes between two or more States.\nElection disputes are not part of this original jurisdiction under Article 131.","revisionPoints":"Article 131 → original jurisdiction of Supreme Court.\nCovers certain disputes involving the Union and States.\nAlso covers disputes between two or more States.\nElection disputes are handled under separate constitutional/statutory provisions."},
{"no":11,"text":"Some species of which of the following are employed as biopesticides?\n1. Bacteria\n2. Fungi\n3. Flowering plants","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biopesticides","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Biopesticides are pest-control agents derived from living organisms or naturally occurring biological materials.\nCertain bacteria, such as Bacillus thuringiensis, are used as biopesticides.\nCertain fungi are also used for biological pest control.\nSome plants and plant-derived substances can have pesticidal properties.","revisionPoints":"Biopesticides → biological agents used to control pests.\nBacillus thuringiensis → important bacterial biopesticide.\nCertain fungi can act as entomopathogens.\nPlant-derived compounds can also be used in pest management."},
{"no":12,"text":"Biomass gasification is considered a sustainable solution to India's power crisis. Which statements are correct?\n1. Coconut shells, groundnut shells and rice husk can be used in biomass gasification.\n2. Combustible gases generated consist of hydrogen and carbon dioxide only.\n3. These gases can be used for direct heat generation but not in internal combustion engines.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Biomass Gasification","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Agricultural residues such as coconut shells, groundnut shells and rice husk can serve as biomass feedstock.\nGasification produces a mixture of gases, commonly including carbon monoxide, hydrogen, methane and carbon dioxide.\nThe resulting producer/syngas can be used in suitable internal combustion engines after appropriate treatment.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Biomass gasification → thermochemical conversion of biomass into combustible gas.\nFeedstocks include agricultural and forestry residues.\nProducer gas may contain CO, H₂, CH₄ and CO₂.\nApplications include heat and electricity generation."},
{"no":13,"text":"What is the role of ultraviolet (UV) radiation in water purification?\n1. It inactivates/kills harmful microorganisms.\n2. It removes all undesirable odours.\n3. It quickens sedimentation, removes turbidity and improves water clarity.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["UV Radiation","Water Purification","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"UV radiation is used mainly for disinfection.\nIt damages the genetic material of microorganisms, preventing them from reproducing.\nUV treatment does not function as a general method for removing turbidity or suspended particles.\nIt is therefore primarily a microbial-control technique.","revisionPoints":"UV water treatment → disinfection.\nIt is effective against many bacteria, viruses and other microorganisms.\nUV does not replace physical filtration where turbidity is high.\nUV treatment generally does not remove dissolved chemicals."},
{"no":14,"text":"Graphene is frequently in the news. What is its importance?\n1. It is a two-dimensional material with good electrical conductivity.\n2. It is one of the thinnest but strongest materials tested.\n3. It is entirely made of silicon and has high optical transparency.\n4. It can be used as conducting electrodes for touch screens, LCDs and organic LEDs.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["Graphene","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Graphene is a single layer of carbon atoms arranged in a hexagonal lattice.\nIt has excellent electrical conductivity and exceptional mechanical strength.\nIt is extremely thin and has useful optical properties.\nStatement 3 is incorrect because graphene is made of carbon, not silicon.","revisionPoints":"Graphene → two-dimensional carbon material.\nExcellent electrical and thermal conductivity.\nVery high mechanical strength relative to its thickness.\nPotential applications → electronics, sensors, batteries and transparent electrodes."},
{"no":15,"text":"After leaded petrol was banned, what are still sources of lead poisoning?\n1. Smelting units\n2. Pens and pencils\n3. Paints\n4. Hair oils and cosmetics","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Lead Poisoning","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Industrial activities such as smelting can release lead.\nLead-containing paints can remain an important exposure source.\nOrdinary pens and pencils are not treated as major lead-poisoning sources in the source.\nTherefore, statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Lead is a toxic heavy metal.\nMajor exposure sources can include industrial emissions and contaminated paints.\nLead exposure can affect the nervous system, especially in children.\nPrevention requires controlling environmental and occupational exposure."},
{"no":16,"text":"With reference to stem cells, which statements are correct?\n1. Stem cells can be derived from mammals only.\n2. Stem cells can be used for screening new drugs.\n3. Stem cells can be used for medical therapies.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Stem Cells","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Stem cells are not restricted to mammals, so statement 1 is incorrect.\nStem cells can be used to create models for drug screening and disease research.\nThey also have applications in regenerative medicine and certain medical therapies.\nTherefore, statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Stem cells → cells capable of self-renewal and differentiation.\nUsed in research and drug screening.\nPotential applications include regenerative medicine.\nMajor types include embryonic and adult/somatic stem cells."},
{"no":17,"text":"Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs), known as ozone-depleting substances, are/were used:\n1. In production of plastic foams\n2. In production of tubeless tyres\n3. In cleaning certain electronic components\n4. As pressurising agents in aerosol cans","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"4 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["Chlorofluorocarbons","Ozone Depletion","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"CFCs were widely used as blowing agents in foam production.\nThey were also used as solvents for cleaning electronic components.\nThey were used as propellants in aerosol products.\nThe source does not include tubeless tyre production as a correct application.","revisionPoints":"CFCs → major ozone-depleting substances.\nCFCs release chlorine in the stratosphere.\nChlorine participates in catalytic destruction of ozone.\nThe Montreal Protocol controls ozone-depleting substances."},
{"no":18,"text":"What are the implications of creating anti-matter such as an anti-helium nucleus?\n1. It will make mineral prospecting and oil exploration easier and cheaper.\n2. It will help investigate the possibility of stars and galaxies made of anti-matter.\n3. It will help understand the evolution of the universe.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Anti-matter","Brookhaven","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Antimatter research helps scientists study the fundamental differences and similarities between matter and antimatter.\nDetection of antimatter in cosmic environments could provide clues about whether antimatter structures exist in the universe.\nSuch research can contribute to understanding the origin and evolution of the universe.\nThe source does not identify mineral prospecting or oil exploration as the relevant implication.","revisionPoints":"Antimatter → particles with properties corresponding to ordinary matter but opposite charges where applicable.\nExample → positron is the antiparticle of the electron.\nMatter-antimatter asymmetry is an important question in cosmology and particle physics.\nAntimatter research helps investigate fundamental properties of the universe."},
{"no":19,"text":"Which of the following are cited as evidence for the continued expansion of the universe?\n1. Detection of microwaves in space\n2. Observation of redshift in space\n3. Movement of asteroids in space\n4. Occurrence of supernova explosions in space","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"None of the above","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Expansion of Universe","Redshift","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The cosmic microwave background provides important evidence concerning the early universe and its evolution.\nThe observation of redshift in distant galaxies demonstrates that distant galaxies are generally receding from us.\nThis redshift is central to the evidence for cosmic expansion.\nThe movement of ordinary asteroids is not evidence for the expansion of the universe.","revisionPoints":"Redshift → key observational evidence for cosmic expansion.\nCosmic Microwave Background (CMB) → remnant radiation from the early universe.\nExpansion of the universe is associated with the Big Bang model.\nDistant galaxies generally show greater recession velocities with increasing distance."},
{"no":20,"text":"Electrically charged particles from space travelling at very high speeds can harm living beings. What prevents them from reaching Earth's surface?","A":"Earth's magnetic field diverts them towards the poles","B":"Ozone layer reflects them back into space","C":"Moisture in the upper atmosphere prevents them","D":"None of the above","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Earth’s Magnetic Field","Cosmic Rays","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Charged particles from the Sun and cosmic environment can be influenced by Earth's magnetic field.\nThe magnetic field deflects many charged particles and guides some toward the polar regions.\nInteraction between charged solar particles and Earth's magnetic field contributes to phenomena such as auroras.\nEarth's magnetic field therefore provides an important layer of protection.","revisionPoints":"Magnetosphere → region dominated by Earth's magnetic field.\nIt helps shield Earth from many charged particles in the solar wind.\nCharged particles entering polar regions can produce aurorae.\nThe atmosphere also provides protection against several forms of radiation."},
{"no":21,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the scientific progress in ancient India:\n1. Specialized surgical instruments were known by the 1st century AD.\n2. Internal organ transplantation was known by the early 3rd century AD.\n3. The concept of sine was known by the 5th century AD.\n4. Cyclic quadrilateral was known by the 7th century AD.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Ancient Indian Science","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Ancient Indian medical texts describe the use of specialized surgical instruments.\nThe concept of internal organ transplantation by the early 3rd century AD is not supported by the source.\nIndian mathematicians were familiar with the concept of sine by the 5th century AD.\nKnowledge of the cyclic quadrilateral is associated with developments in ancient Indian mathematics.","revisionPoints":"Ancient India made significant contributions to medicine and mathematics.\nSurgical instruments are described in early Indian medical traditions.\nSine was an important development in Indian trigonometry.\nGeometry developed alongside astronomy and mathematics."},
{"no":22,"text":"Which of the following are common features of Buddhism and Jainism?\n1. Indifference to the authority of the Vedas\n2. Denial of the efficacy of rituals\n3. Avoidance of extremes in life\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","Jainism","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Both Buddhism and Jainism rejected the exclusive authority of the Vedas.\nBoth questioned the effectiveness of ritualistic practices as the primary means of spiritual liberation.\nAvoidance of extremes is specifically associated with Buddhism's Middle Path.\nJainism traditionally emphasizes rigorous austerities, so statement 3 is not a common feature.","revisionPoints":"Buddhism and Jainism emerged as important Śramaṇa traditions.\nBoth challenged aspects of Vedic ritualism.\nBuddhism → Middle Path.\nJainism → strong emphasis on asceticism and austerity."},
{"no":23,"text":"Which of the following are associated with Inclusive Governance?\n1. Effective District Planning Committees\n2. Increased public health expenditure\n3. Strengthening of the Mid-day Meal Scheme\n4. Permitting NBFCs to undertake banking activities\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Inclusive Governance","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Inclusive governance seeks to ensure that development benefits reach disadvantaged and vulnerable groups.\nIncreased expenditure on public health promotes wider access to essential services.\nStrengthening the Mid-day Meal Scheme supports nutrition and education among children.\nThe source identifies these measures within the framework of inclusive governance.","revisionPoints":"Inclusive governance focuses on access, participation and equitable development.\nPublic health is an important component of social inclusion.\nNutrition programmes can improve both health and educational participation.\nDistrict-level planning can support decentralisation."},
{"no":24,"text":"The terms Nagara, Dravida and Vesara are associated with:","A":"Three main styles of Indian classical dance","B":"Three major schools of Indian philosophy","C":"Three main styles of Indian temple architecture","D":"Three major styles of Indian miniature painting","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Temples & Shrines","tags":["Nagara","Dravida","Vesara","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Nagara is broadly associated with northern Indian temple architecture.\nDravida is associated mainly with southern India.\nVesara represents a style generally associated with the Deccan and incorporates features of Nagara and Dravida traditions.","revisionPoints":"Nagara → North India\nDravida → South India\nVesara → Deccan/combined characteristics\nThese classifications relate to temple architecture."},
{"no":25,"text":"Why did the Congress ministries resign from office in the provinces in 1939?","A":"Failure of the British Government to grant dominion status","B":"Rejection of the demand for a Constituent Assembly","C":"Failure of the British Government to release political prisoners","D":"India's involvement in the Second World War was declared by the British Government without consulting Indian leaders","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Congress Ministries 1939","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Britain declared war on Germany in September 1939.\nIndia was declared a participant in the war by the British Government.\nIndian leaders were not consulted before this decision.\nIn protest, the Congress ministries resigned from the provincial governments.","revisionPoints":"1937: Congress formed ministries in several provinces.\n1939: Congress ministries resigned.\nImmediate context → India's entry into World War II without Indian consultation.\nThe resignation intensified the political confrontation between Congress and British rule."},
{"no":26,"text":"Which of the following are functions of an ASHA worker?\n1. Creating awareness about health and healthy practices\n2. Facilitating access to health services\n3. Providing counselling on maternal and child health\n4. Conducting deliveries independently\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["NRHM","ASHA","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"ASHA stands for Accredited Social Health Activist.\nASHA workers act as a link between the community and the public health system.\nThey create awareness and facilitate access to healthcare.\nThey provide counselling related to maternal and child health.\nConducting deliveries independently is not their stated function in the source.","revisionPoints":"ASHA is a community-level health worker.\nActs as an interface between community and health services.\nImportant in maternal and child health.\nPart of India's broader public health and community outreach system."},
{"no":27,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Government of India Act, 1919:\n1. It introduced dyarchy in the provinces.\n2. It introduced separate communal electorates for Muslims.\n3. It provided for the devolution of legislative authority to the provinces.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Government of India Act 1919","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Government of India Act, 1919 introduced dyarchy in the provinces.\nIt provided for greater devolution of legislative authority to provinces.\nSeparate electorates for Muslims had already been introduced by the Indian Councils Act, 1909.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1919 → Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms\nIntroduced dyarchy in provinces.\nSubjects were divided into reserved and transferred.\nSeparate Muslim electorates → 1909, not 1919."},
{"no":28,"text":"The National Social Conference was formed because:","A":"The British Government banned discussion of social issues in the Congress","B":"The Indian National Congress did not want social reform issues to be included in its deliberations","C":"Social reformers wanted to establish a political party","D":"The Congress rejected all social reform movements","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["National Social Conference","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Political and social reform were considered related but distinct areas.\nThe National Social Conference provided a separate platform for discussing social reform.\nIt was associated with reformers who wanted issues such as social customs and reform to receive organized attention.","revisionPoints":"National Social Conference → social reform platform.\nReflects the interaction between political nationalism and social reform.\nSocial reform debates included issues concerning social customs and practices.\nImportant for understanding the broader 19th-century reform movement."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which of the following organisations were associated with Dr. B. R. Ambedkar?\n1. All India Scheduled Castes Federation\n2. Independent Labour Party\n3. Swaraj Party\n4. Justice Party\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["B.R. Ambedkar","Political Parties","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Dr. B. R. Ambedkar founded the Independent Labour Party in 1936.\nHe later established the All India Scheduled Castes Federation.\nThe Swaraj Party and Justice Party were separate political organisations.","revisionPoints":"Independent Labour Party → 1936\nAll India Scheduled Castes Federation → Ambedkar\nAmbedkar's political activities focused strongly on social equality and representation.\nHis organisations sought political and social empowerment of marginalised communities."},
{"no":30,"text":"Which of the following are special powers of the Rajya Sabha?\n1. It can authorise Parliament to legislate on a subject in the State List in the national interest.\n2. It can authorise the creation of one or more All India Services common to the Union and States.\n3. It can initiate a Money Bill.\n4. It can remove the President by impeachment.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Rajya Sabha Special Powers","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Under Article 249, Rajya Sabha can authorise Parliament to legislate on a State List subject in the national interest.\nUnder Article 312, Rajya Sabha can authorise creation of new All India Services.\nA Money Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha.\nImpeachment of the President requires participation of both Houses.","revisionPoints":"Rajya Sabha represents the States and Union Territories.\nArticle 249 → State List legislation in national interest.\nArticle 312 → All India Services.\nMoney Bills can originate only in Lok Sabha."},
{"no":31,"text":"The National Rural Livelihood Mission primarily seeks to improve rural livelihoods through:","A":"Expansion of large-scale industries","B":"Strengthening Self-Help Groups and skill development","C":"Increasing agricultural exports only","D":"Establishing urban employment centres","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["National Rural Livelihood Mission","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The mission focuses on improving the livelihoods of rural poor households.\nSelf-Help Groups (SHGs) are an important institutional mechanism.\nSkill development and livelihood opportunities support income generation.\nThe approach emphasizes community-based institutions and sustainable livelihoods.","revisionPoints":"NRLM → rural poverty reduction and livelihood promotion.\nSHGs are central to the approach.\nIncludes skill development and livelihood diversification.\nFocuses particularly on rural poor households."},
{"no":32,"text":"The Multidimensional Poverty Index measures deprivation in which of the following areas?","A":"Education, health, assets and services","B":"Income, exports and taxation only","C":"Agricultural production and industrial output","D":"Population growth and migration only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Multidimensional Poverty Index","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Multidimensional poverty goes beyond income-based poverty.\nIt captures deprivation across multiple dimensions of household well-being.\nThese include education, health, living conditions, assets and access to services.\nThus, poverty can exist even when income alone does not show the full extent of deprivation.","revisionPoints":"MPI ≠ income poverty alone.\nUses multiple dimensions of deprivation.\nImportant dimensions → health, education and living standards.\nHelps identify the different forms of poverty experienced by households."},
{"no":33,"text":"Which of the following were notable recommendations of the 13th Finance Commission?\n1. Recommendations related to the design of GST\n2. A compensation package related to GST\n3. Specified share of central taxes to local bodies as grants\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["13th Finance Commission","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The 13th Finance Commission dealt with important issues of fiscal federalism.\nIts recommendations included aspects related to the proposed GST framework.\nIt also considered a compensation mechanism associated with GST.\nIt provided for grants to local bodies linked to a specified share of central tax revenues.","revisionPoints":"Finance Commission → Article 280.\nDeals with Union-State fiscal relations.\nRecommends tax devolution and grants-in-aid.\n13th Finance Commission was important in discussions surrounding GST and local government finances."},
{"no":34,"text":"Which of the following are examples of manufacturing policy initiatives?\n1. National Investment and Manufacturing Zones\n2. Single-window clearance mechanisms\n3. Technology Acquisition and Development Fund\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["NIMZ","Manufacturing Policy","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"NIMZs were proposed to promote large-scale manufacturing investment.\nSingle-window mechanisms aim to simplify regulatory approvals.\nThe Technology Acquisition and Development Fund was designed to support technological capabilities.\nTogether, these measures address investment, ease of doing business and technology development.","revisionPoints":"NIMZ → manufacturing investment and infrastructure.\nSingle-window clearance → simplification of approvals.\nTechnology Acquisition Fund → technological development.\nManufacturing policy seeks to improve India's industrial competitiveness."},
{"no":35,"text":"Which of the following are instruments through which Parliament exercises control over public finance?\n1. Annual Financial Statement\n2. Appropriation Bill\n3. Supplementary grants and vote-on-account\n4. Finance Bill\n5. Parliamentary Budget Office\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4 only","B":"1, 3 and 5 only","C":"2, 4 and 5 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Parliamentary Control over Finance","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Annual Financial Statement presents the government's estimated receipts and expenditure.\nThe Appropriation Bill authorises withdrawal from the Consolidated Fund of India.\nSupplementary grants deal with additional expenditure requirements.\nA vote-on-account allows expenditure for a limited period before the full budget is passed.\nThe Finance Bill gives effect to taxation proposals.\nA Parliamentary Budget Office is not one of the listed constitutional parliamentary budget instruments in the source.","revisionPoints":"Annual Financial Statement → Article 112.\nAppropriation → authorises withdrawal from the Consolidated Fund of India.\nVote-on-account → temporary expenditure authority.\nFinance Bill → gives effect to taxation proposals."},
{"no":36,"text":"Mahatma Gandhi undertook a fast unto death in 1932 mainly in response to:","A":"Simon Commission","B":"Government of India Act, 1935","C":"Communal Award announced by Ramsay MacDonald","D":"Cripps Mission","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Mahatma Gandhi","Communal Award","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Communal Award was announced by British Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald in 1932.\nIt provided separate electorates for various communities, including the Depressed Classes.\nGandhi strongly opposed separate electorates for the Depressed Classes.\nHis fast ultimately led to negotiations resulting in the Poona Pact of 1932.","revisionPoints":"Communal Award → 1932\nAnnounced by Ramsay MacDonald.\nGandhi's fast → opposition to separate electorates for the Depressed Classes.\nPoona Pact → 1932."},
{"no":37,"text":"Which of the following statements correctly describe the Ryotwari system?\n1. Land revenue was collected directly from the cultivators.\n2. Pattas were issued to the ryots.\n3. Land was surveyed and assessed before fixing the revenue.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Ryotwari Settlement","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Under the Ryotwari system, the cultivator (ryot) dealt directly with the government.\nPattas documented the cultivator's landholding and revenue obligation.\nLand was surveyed and its revenue liability was assessed.\nThe system was particularly associated with parts of Madras and Bombay Presidencies.","revisionPoints":"Ryotwari → direct settlement with cultivator.\nMajor names associated → Thomas Munro and Alexander Read.\nDifferent from Permanent Settlement → no zamindar intermediary as the revenue-collecting landholder.\nImportant in southern and western parts of British India."},
{"no":38,"text":"Which sequence correctly represents the stages of demographic transition?","A":"High birth rate/high death rate → low birth rate/low death rate → high birth rate/low death rate","B":"Low birth rate/low death rate → high birth rate/high death rate → high birth rate/low death rate","C":"High birth rate/high death rate → high birth rate/low death rate → low birth rate/low death rate","D":"High birth rate/low death rate → high birth rate/high death rate → low birth rate/low death rate","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Demographic Transition","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"In the initial stage, both birth and death rates are high.\nImprovements in sanitation, nutrition and healthcare reduce mortality first.\nBirth rates remain high for some time, producing rapid population growth.\nLater, fertility declines and both birth and death rates become low.","revisionPoints":"Stage 1 → high birth + high death rates.\nStage 2 → death rate falls while birth rate remains high.\nStage 3 → birth rate also declines.\nPopulation growth is generally fastest during the high birth–low death phase."},
{"no":39,"text":"Which of the following are included among India's Eight Core Industries?\n1. Cement\n2. Fertilizers\n3. Natural Gas\n4. Refinery Products\n5. Textiles\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2, 3 and 5 only","C":"1, 2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Eight Core Industries","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The eight core industries include Cement, Fertilizers, Natural Gas and Refinery Products.\nTextiles is not one of the eight core industries.\nThe core industries have major significance for India's industrial production index.","revisionPoints":"The Eight Core Industries are:\nCoal\nCrude Oil\nNatural Gas\nRefinery Products\nFertilizers\nSteel\nCement\nElectricity\nTogether they account for a significant share of the Index of Industrial Production (IIP)."},
{"no":40,"text":"Which of the following constitutional provisions have a bearing on education in India?\n1. Directive Principles of State Policy\n2. Provisions relating to local bodies\n3. Fifth Schedule\n4. Sixth Schedule\n5. Seventh Schedule\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3, 4 and 5","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Education","Constitutional Provisions","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Education is addressed through constitutional provisions at multiple levels.\nThe Directive Principles establish broader educational and social objectives.\nLocal bodies have responsibilities connected with local development and education.\nThe Fifth and Sixth Schedules contain provisions relevant to administration in Scheduled and tribal areas.\nThe Seventh Schedule deals with distribution of legislative powers between the Union and States.","revisionPoints":"Education is connected with Fundamental Rights, DPSPs and legislative powers.\nFifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes.\nSixth Schedule → Tribal Areas in specified northeastern states.\nSeventh Schedule → Union, State and Concurrent Lists."},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Sea Buckthorn:\n1. It helps control soil erosion and desertification.\n2. It has nutritional value and is adapted to cold, high-altitude regions.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Sea Buckthorn","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Sea buckthorn is a hardy plant suited to cold and high-altitude environments.\nIts extensive root system helps in soil stabilisation and erosion control.\nIt has nutritional value and contains various bioactive compounds.\nIt is therefore relevant to both ecological restoration and livelihood opportunities in cold regions.","revisionPoints":"Sea buckthorn → adapted to cold, high-altitude areas.\nHelps control soil erosion and desertification.\nHas nutritional and economic value.\nCan contribute to ecological restoration."},
{"no":42,"text":"What is meant by mixed farming?","A":"Cultivation of two or more crops together","B":"Cultivation of crops in different seasons","C":"Rearing animals and cultivation of crops together","D":"Cultivation of crops using both irrigation and rainwater","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Mixed Farming","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Mixed farming combines crop cultivation with livestock rearing.\nCrop residues can provide feed for animals.\nAnimal manure can contribute nutrients to agricultural fields.\nIt allows farmers to diversify sources of income.","revisionPoints":"Mixed farming = crops + livestock.\nPromotes recycling of farm resources.\nCan diversify farm income.\nDifferent from mixed cropping, which refers to growing multiple crops together."},
{"no":43,"text":"Which of the following States is located approximately at the same latitude as northern Rajasthan and has more than 80% forest cover and more than 12% of its area under the protected area network?","A":"Arunachal Pradesh","B":"Sikkim","C":"Meghalaya","D":"Nagaland","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Arunachal Pradesh","Forest Cover","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Arunachal Pradesh is situated in the northeastern part of India.\nIt has extensive forest cover.\nIts mountainous terrain supports rich biodiversity.\nA significant portion of the State is covered by protected areas.","revisionPoints":"Arunachal Pradesh → Eastern Himalayas.\nOne of India's major forest-rich States.\nHigh biodiversity due to varied altitude and climate.\nImportant region for Himalayan ecosystems and wildlife."},
{"no":44,"text":"Cowpea, green gram and pigeon pea are commonly used for:","A":"Fibre production","B":"Oil extraction","C":"Beverage production","D":"Fodder, feed and green manure","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Cowpea","Green Gram","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"These leguminous crops can be used as fodder/feed.\nLegumes are important in agricultural systems because of their association with nitrogen fixation.\nThey can also contribute organic matter and nutrients when used as green manure.\nTheir multiple uses make them valuable components of sustainable farming systems.","revisionPoints":"Cowpea → important pulse/fodder crop.\nGreen gram → commonly known as moong.\nPigeon pea → commonly known as arhar/tur.\nLegumes are associated with nitrogen fixation."},
{"no":45,"text":"Ocean currents are influenced by which of the following?\n1. Rotation of the Earth\n2. Air pressure and winds\n3. Density of ocean water\n4. Revolution of the Earth\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Ocean Currents","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Earth's rotation produces the Coriolis effect, influencing ocean-current direction.\nWinds and differences in atmospheric pressure drive surface currents.\nDifferences in seawater temperature and salinity create density differences that contribute to deep-water circulation.\nRevolution around the Sun is not identified as a direct factor in the source.","revisionPoints":"Surface currents → strongly influenced by winds.\nCoriolis effect → consequence of Earth's rotation.\nThermohaline circulation → linked to temperature and salinity.\nOcean currents influence climate and marine ecosystems."},
{"no":46,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding wetlands in India:\n1. Gujarat has the largest area under wetlands among Indian States.\n2. Coastal wetlands occupy a larger area than inland wetlands in India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["Wetlands of India","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"According to the source, Gujarat has the largest geographical area under wetlands among Indian States.\nThe statement that coastal wetlands occupy a larger area than inland wetlands is not correct according to the source.\nWetlands include a wide range of inland and coastal ecosystems.","revisionPoints":"Wetlands include lakes, marshes, mangroves, estuaries and other wet ecosystems.\nThey provide habitat for numerous species.\nWetlands contribute to flood regulation and water purification.\nGujarat has a significant extent of wetland ecosystems."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following crops are predominantly rainfed?\n1. Groundnut\n2. Sesamum\n3. Pearl millet\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Rainfed Crops","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Groundnut, sesamum and pearl millet are important dryland/rainfed crops.\nThey can be cultivated under relatively limited irrigation.\nTheir cultivation is closely linked to monsoon rainfall in many parts of India.\nRainfed agriculture is particularly important in semi-arid regions.","revisionPoints":"Groundnut → important oilseed crop.\nSesamum → important oilseed.\nPearl millet → bajra, drought-tolerant cereal.\nRainfed agriculture is highly dependent on monsoon variability."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following features provide evidence that the Himalayas are young fold mountains?\n1. Deep gorges\n2. U-turn river courses\n3. Parallel mountain ranges\n4. Steep gradients and frequent landslides\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Himalayas","Fold Mountains","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Himalayas are geologically young and continue to experience tectonic activity.\nRivers have carved deep gorges through rapidly rising terrain.\nComplex geological structure produces parallel ranges and unusual river courses.\nSteep slopes contribute to landslides and other geomorphic processes.","revisionPoints":"Himalayas → young fold mountains.\nFormed mainly through convergence of the Indian and Eurasian plates.\nCharacterised by active tectonics and high relief.\nLandslides and earthquakes are important Himalayan hazards."},
{"no":49,"text":"Why does temperature generally decrease with increasing altitude in the troposphere?","A":"The atmosphere becomes denser with height","B":"Solar radiation becomes completely absent at higher altitudes","C":"The atmosphere is heated mainly from the Earth's surface and upper air is less dense","D":"Earth's gravitational force disappears at higher altitudes","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Atmospheric Temperature","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Earth's surface absorbs solar energy and then heats the atmosphere from below.\nAs altitude increases, atmospheric pressure and density generally decrease.\nThe troposphere therefore generally becomes colder with height.\nThis vertical temperature decrease is called the environmental lapse rate.","revisionPoints":"Troposphere → lowest layer of the atmosphere.\nTemperature generally decreases with altitude.\nAverage environmental lapse rate → about 6.5°C per km.\nMost weather phenomena occur in the troposphere."},
{"no":50,"text":"Ocean acidification is a concern because it can affect:\n1. Calcareous phytoplankton\n2. Coral reefs\n3. Animals whose larvae depend on phytoplankton\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Ocean Acidification","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Increased atmospheric CO₂ is absorbed by oceans.\nThis changes seawater chemistry and lowers ocean pH.\nReduced carbonate availability can affect organisms that form calcium carbonate structures.\nCoral reefs and certain planktonic organisms are particularly vulnerable.","revisionPoints":"Ocean acidification → linked to increasing CO₂ absorption by oceans.\nAffects carbonate chemistry.\nCoral reefs are vulnerable.\nCan have cascading effects throughout marine food webs."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which of the following statements correctly describe the significance of the Comptroller and Auditor General (CAG) of India?\n1. The Public Accounts Committee examines CAG reports.\n2. Investigating agencies can use information contained in CAG reports.\n3. The CAG directly controls the exchequer during national emergencies.\n4. The CAG possesses judicial and prosecutorial powers.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["Comptroller and Auditor General","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The CAG audits government receipts and expenditure.\nReports of the CAG are examined by parliamentary committees, particularly the Public Accounts Committee (PAC).\nInformation emerging from audit reports can be relevant to investigating agencies.\nThe CAG is an auditing authority, not a judicial or prosecutorial body.","revisionPoints":"CAG → constitutional authority under Article 148.\nAudits Union and State government accounts as provided by law.\nCAG reports are laid before the legislature.\nPAC examines important CAG reports."},
{"no":52,"text":"The Janani Suraksha Yojana (JSY) primarily aims to:\n1. Promote institutional deliveries\n2. Provide monetary assistance to mothers for delivery\n3. Compensate women for loss of wages during pregnancy\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Janani Suraksha Yojana","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"JSY is a maternal health intervention.\nIt seeks to encourage institutional delivery.\nEligible women receive cash assistance under the scheme.\nThe source does not identify compensation for wage loss as its primary objective.","revisionPoints":"JSY → maternal health programme.\nFocus → institutional deliveries.\nProvides cash assistance to eligible beneficiaries.\nAssociated with reducing maternal and neonatal risks."},
{"no":53,"text":"With regard to the appointment of the Prime Minister of India, which of the following is correct?","A":"The Prime Minister must necessarily be a Member of Parliament at the time of appointment.","B":"The Prime Minister can never be appointed from outside Parliament.","C":"A person who is not a Member of Parliament can be appointed Prime Minister but must become a member of either House within six months.","D":"Only a sitting Lok Sabha member can become Prime Minister.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Prime Minister","Parliament Membership","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Constitution does not require the Prime Minister to already be an MP at the moment of appointment.\nA non-member may be appointed.\nHowever, the person must become a member of either House of Parliament within six months.\nOtherwise, the person ceases to hold the ministerial office.","revisionPoints":"Prime Minister is appointed by the President.\nCouncil of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha.\nA minister can remain outside Parliament for a maximum of six months.\nThe Prime Minister can belong to Lok Sabha or Rajya Sabha."},
{"no":54,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Delimitation Commission:\n1. Its orders cannot be challenged in a court of law.\n2. Neither Parliament nor a State Legislature can modify its orders once laid before them.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Delimitation Commission","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Delimitation Commission is established to redraw electoral constituencies.\nIts orders have a special constitutional/statutory protection against judicial challenge.\nOnce laid before the legislature, the orders cannot ordinarily be modified by Parliament or State Legislatures.\nThis is intended to preserve the independence of the delimitation process.","revisionPoints":"Delimitation → redrawing of electoral constituencies.\nAims to reflect population changes.\nDelimitation Commission works independently.\nIts orders receive special legal protection."},
{"no":55,"text":"Under the Employees' State Insurance (ESI) scheme, coverage can extend to employees of which of the following establishments?\n1. Hotels and restaurants\n2. Motor transport undertakings\n3. Newspaper establishments\n4. Private medical institutions\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["Employees State Insurance Scheme","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The ESI framework provides social security and health protection to eligible employees.\nCoverage extends beyond factories to several categories of establishments.\nThese include hotels/restaurants, motor transport establishments, newspapers and certain medical institutions.\nApplicability depends on the conditions prescribed under the relevant law and notifications.","revisionPoints":"ESI → social security scheme for eligible employees.\nProvides benefits including medical and cash benefits.\nImplemented through the Employees' State Insurance Corporation (ESIC).\nCoverage can extend to establishments beyond manufacturing industries."},
{"no":56,"text":"Which of the following reports/recommendations are laid before Parliament by the President?\n1. Finance Commission recommendations\n2. Report of the CAG\n3. Report of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes\n4. Report of the Public Accounts Committee\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2, 3 and 4","D":"2 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["President","Reports Laid Before Parliament","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Constitution provides for presentation of several constitutional bodies' reports before Parliament.\nFinance Commission recommendations are laid before Parliament along with an explanatory memorandum.\nCAG reports are submitted to the President, who causes them to be laid before Parliament.\nReports of the National Commission for Scheduled Castes are also laid before Parliament.\nThe Public Accounts Committee is a parliamentary committee and its report is not laid by the President.","revisionPoints":"Finance Commission → Article 280.\nCAG → Article 148.\nNCSC → Article 338.\nParliamentary committees function within the legislative process, unlike constitutional commissions."},
{"no":57,"text":"In which of the following situations can a joint sitting of Parliament be convened?","A":"Deadlock between Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha over an ordinary Bill","B":"Deadlock over a Money Bill","C":"Deadlock over a Constitutional Amendment Bill","D":"Whenever Rajya Sabha rejects a Money Bill","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Joint Sitting","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"A joint sitting can be used when the two Houses disagree over an ordinary legislative Bill.\nIt can arise when one House rejects the Bill or when there is disagreement over amendments, subject to constitutional conditions.\nJoint sitting is not available for Money Bills.\nIt is also not available for Constitution Amendment Bills.","revisionPoints":"Joint sitting → Article 108.\nPresided over by the Speaker of Lok Sabha.\nNot applicable to Money Bills.\nNot applicable to Constitution Amendment Bills."},
{"no":58,"text":"The District Rural Development Agencies (DRDAs) were mainly associated with:\n1. Inter-sectoral coordination\n2. Ensuring effective utilisation of funds for anti-poverty programmes\n3. Conducting foreign trade promotion\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["District Rural Development Agencies","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"DRDAs were established to coordinate and monitor rural development and anti-poverty programmes.\nThey promoted coordination among different sectors and agencies.\nEnsuring effective use of funds was an important responsibility.\nForeign trade promotion was unrelated to their core rural-development role.","revisionPoints":"DRDA → rural development administration.\nFocused on implementation and coordination of anti-poverty programmes.\nImportant role in fund utilisation and monitoring.\nConnected with district-level rural development planning."},
{"no":59,"text":"Which of the following are Fundamental Duties under the Constitution of India?\n1. To preserve the rich heritage of India's composite culture\n2. To develop scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform\n3. To strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity\n4. To promote the educational and economic interests of weaker sections\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fundamental Duties","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Fundamental Duties are contained in Article 51A.\nPreserving India's composite cultural heritage is a Fundamental Duty.\nDeveloping scientific temper and striving for excellence are also Fundamental Duties.\nPromoting the educational and economic interests of weaker sections is a Directive Principle, not a Fundamental Duty.","revisionPoints":"Fundamental Duties → Part IVA.\nAdded by the 42nd Constitutional Amendment, 1976.\nOriginally contained 10 duties; the 11th was added later.\nArticle 51A contains the Fundamental Duties."},
{"no":60,"text":"Which of the following provisions help safeguard the independence of the Supreme Court?\n1. The President consults the Chief Justice of India in appointing Supreme Court judges.\n2. Salaries of Supreme Court judges are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.\n3. Salaries of Supreme Court judges are subject to annual voting by Parliament.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Supreme Court Autonomy","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The constitutional appointment process involves consultation with the Chief Justice of India.\nSalaries and allowances of Supreme Court judges are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.\nCharged expenditure is not submitted to Parliament for voting.\nTherefore, statement 3 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Supreme Court → highest judicial authority in India.\nJudicial independence is supported through security of tenure and financial safeguards.\nJudges' salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.\nThe constitutional framework seeks to protect courts from executive and legislative pressure.\nTop of Form\nBottom of Form"},
{"no":61,"text":"Which of the following are advantages of thorium as a nuclear fuel?\n1. Thorium is more abundant than uranium.\n2. It can produce more energy per unit mass of mined mineral.\n3. It produces less harmful radioactive waste.\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["Thorium","Uranium","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Thorium is relatively more abundant in the Earth's crust than uranium.\nThe thorium fuel cycle can potentially yield substantial energy from mined material.\nCompared with conventional uranium fuel cycles, thorium-based systems can have advantages regarding the quantity and characteristics of long-lived radioactive waste.\nThorium-232 itself is fertile rather than directly fissile and requires conversion to uranium-233 for a sustained fuel cycle.","revisionPoints":"Thorium-232 → fertile material.\nCan be converted into Uranium-233.\nIndia has significant thorium resources.\nThorium is important in India's long-term nuclear-energy strategy."},
{"no":62,"text":"Why does carbon dioxide increase atmospheric temperature?","A":"It reflects all incoming solar radiation","B":"It absorbs ultraviolet radiation from the Sun","C":"It blocks the movement of air in the atmosphere","D":"It absorbs infrared radiation emitted by the Earth's surface","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Carbon Dioxide","Infrared Radiation","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Earth's surface absorbs incoming solar energy and emits energy mainly as infrared radiation.\nGreenhouse gases such as CO₂ absorb part of this outgoing infrared radiation.\nThe absorbed energy is re-emitted in different directions, including back toward the Earth's surface.\nThis contributes to the greenhouse effect.","revisionPoints":"CO₂ → major greenhouse gas.\nGreenhouse gases absorb outgoing infrared radiation.\nEnhanced greenhouse effect contributes to global warming.\nOther greenhouse gases include methane, nitrous oxide and water vapour."},
{"no":63,"text":"Which of the following elements are primarily responsible for the origin of life?","A":"Carbon, oxygen and calcium","B":"Carbon, hydrogen and nitrogen","C":"Hydrogen, oxygen and sodium","D":"Carbon, silicon and iron","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Origin of Life","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Carbon can form stable and complex molecular structures.\nHydrogen is present in water and numerous organic compounds.\nNitrogen is an essential component of proteins and nucleic acids.\nTogether with oxygen, phosphorus and sulfur, these elements form the major chemical basis of living systems.","revisionPoints":"Major biological elements include C, H, O, N, P and S.\nCarbon is central to organic chemistry.\nNitrogen is essential for amino acids and nucleic acids.\nWater is fundamental to known life."},
{"no":64,"text":"With reference to Bt brinjal, consider the following statements:\n1. It contains a gene derived from a soil bacterium.\n2. It is designed to provide resistance against certain insect pests.\n3. It is developed to improve drought tolerance.\n4. The source associates the technology with the use of a terminator-seed concept.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct according to the source?","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Bt Brinjal","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Bt brinjal is a genetically engineered crop designed primarily for insect resistance.\nThe Bt gene originates from the soil bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis.\nThe source does not identify drought tolerance as the relevant purpose here.\nThe source associates the question with the terminator-seed concept.","revisionPoints":"Bt → Bacillus thuringiensis.\nBt crops are designed to provide resistance against certain insect pests.\nGenetic engineering can introduce specific traits into crops.\nAlways distinguish insect resistance from traits such as drought tolerance."},
{"no":65,"text":"Genetically engineered plants have been developed for which of the following purposes?\n1. Withstanding drought\n2. Increasing nutritional value\n3. Increasing shelf life\n4. Facilitating photosynthesis in spaceships\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Genetically Engineered Plants","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Genetic engineering can introduce traits that improve stress tolerance.\nCrops can be modified to increase nutritional content.\nGenetic modification can also improve characteristics related to storage and shelf life.\nPhotosynthesis in spaceships is not the agricultural application identified in the source.","revisionPoints":"Genetic engineering → introduction/modification of specific genetic traits.\nApplications include stress tolerance.\nCan improve nutritional characteristics.\nCan modify post-harvest characteristics, including shelf life."},
{"no":66,"text":"What was the major contribution of Dadabhai Naoroji to the Indian national movement?","A":"Exposing the economic exploitation of India by British rule","B":"Establishing the first Indian revolutionary organisation","C":"Leading the Quit India Movement","D":"Establishing the Swaraj Party","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Dadabhai Naoroji","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Dadabhai Naoroji systematically analysed the economic consequences of colonial rule.\nHis famous Drain of Wealth theory argued that wealth was being transferred from India to Britain.\nHis economic critique became an important intellectual foundation of Indian nationalism.\nHe was also one of the early leaders of the Indian National Congress.","revisionPoints":"Dadabhai Naoroji → \"Grand Old Man of India\".\nDeveloped the Drain of Wealth theory.\nAuthor of Poverty and Un-British Rule in India.\nServed as President of the Indian National Congress multiple times."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding Dhrupad:\n1. Dhrupad is primarily associated with secular themes.\n2. Dhrupad has strong devotional and spiritual associations.\n3. Its alap traditionally uses syllables associated with Sanskrit mantras.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Dhrupad","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Dhrupad is one of the oldest surviving forms of Hindustani classical vocal music.\nIt has historically had strong devotional and spiritual associations.\nThe alap may use syllables such as nom, tom, associated with traditional vocal practice.\nThe statement describing it as primarily secular is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Dhrupad → important form of Hindustani classical music.\nKnown for its serious and meditative character.\nTraditionally associated with devotional/spiritual themes.\nAlap forms an important part of Dhrupad performance."},
{"no":68,"text":"What distinguishes Kuchipudi from Bharatanatyam?","A":"Kuchipudi performers may occasionally speak dialogues during the performance, unlike Bharatanatyam","B":"Bharatanatyam uses no hand gestures","C":"Kuchipudi has no connection with drama","D":"Bharatanatyam is exclusively performed by male dancers","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["Kuchipudi","Bharatanatyam","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Kuchipudi developed with strong connections to dance-drama traditions.\nPerformers may incorporate spoken dialogue into certain presentations.\nBharatanatyam also uses expressive storytelling but is primarily structured through dance, gesture and abhinaya.\nBoth are major Indian classical dance traditions.","revisionPoints":"Kuchipudi → associated with Andhra Pradesh.\nBharatanatyam → associated with Tamil Nadu.\nBoth use abhinaya, mudras and rhythmic movement.\nKuchipudi has a strong dance-drama tradition."},
{"no":69,"text":"Which of the following practices are associated with Sufi traditions?\n1. Meditation and control of breath\n2. Severe ascetic exercises\n3. Recitation of sacred songs to induce spiritual ecstasy\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Sufi Mystics","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Various Sufi traditions developed practices aimed at spiritual purification and closeness to God.\nMeditation and controlled breathing could be part of spiritual disciplines.\nAscetic practices were followed by some Sufi orders.\nSama, involving devotional music or recitation, was used by certain traditions to induce spiritual states.","revisionPoints":"Sufism → mystical tradition within Islamic religious culture.\nImportant concepts → spiritual purification and closeness to God.\nSilsila → Sufi order/lineage.\nSama → devotional listening/music in certain Sufi traditions."},
{"no":70,"text":"The Rowlatt Act of 1919 was primarily aimed at:","A":"Expanding voting rights","B":"Allowing imprisonment without trial and using summary procedures","C":"Introducing provincial autonomy","D":"Establishing separate electorates for women","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Rowlatt Act","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Rowlatt Act extended extraordinary powers to the colonial government.\nIt permitted detention and other restrictive measures against suspected revolutionary activities.\nIt was widely opposed by Indian political leaders.\nGandhi launched the Rowlatt Satyagraha against the legislation.","revisionPoints":"Rowlatt Act → 1919.\nAssociated with repressive colonial measures.\nGandhi launched the Rowlatt Satyagraha.\nThe period also witnessed the Jallianwala Bagh massacre in April 1919."},
{"no":71,"text":"Why was the Lahore Session of the Indian National Congress in 1929 particularly important?","A":"Congress declared the goal of complete independence","B":"Congress accepted the Cripps proposals","C":"Congress launched the Quit India Movement","D":"Congress accepted the Government of India Act, 1935","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Lahore Session","Purna Swaraj","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Lahore Session was held in December 1929.\nIt was presided over by Jawaharlal Nehru.\nCongress adopted Purna Swaraj as its goal.\n26 January 1930 was observed as Independence Day to give effect to this declaration.","revisionPoints":"Lahore Session → 1929.\nPresident → Jawaharlal Nehru.\nGoal → Purna Swaraj.\n26 January 1930 → observed as Independence Day by Congress."},
{"no":72,"text":"The Bhumisparsha Mudra of the Buddha symbolises:","A":"Teaching the first sermon","B":"Calling the Earth to witness his purity and resistance to Mara","C":"Meditation under the Bodhi tree","D":"Attainment of Nirvana after death","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Bhumisparsha Mudra","Buddha Iconography","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"In the Bhumisparsha or \"Earth-touching\" mudra, the Buddha touches the ground with his right hand.\nIt represents the moment when he called the Earth to bear witness to his spiritual attainment.\nThe gesture is closely associated with the Buddha's enlightenment.","revisionPoints":"Bhumisparsha → Earth-touching gesture.\nAssociated with Buddha's enlightenment.\nCommonly represented with the Buddha seated in meditation.\nMudras communicate specific symbolic meanings in Buddhist art."},
{"no":73,"text":"What was the nature of religion among the early Vedic Aryans?","A":"Predominantly temple-based worship","B":"Worship centred on images of gods","C":"Worship of natural forces along with yajnas","D":"Exclusive worship of a single supreme deity","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Vedic Religion","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Early Vedic religion was closely connected with natural forces and deities.\nImportant deities included Indra, Agni, Varuna and Surya.\nRitual sacrifices or yajnas were important religious practices.\nTemples and image worship did not form the central feature of early Vedic religious practice.","revisionPoints":"Early Vedic religion → nature-oriented.\nImportant deities → Indra, Agni, Varuna, Surya.\nYajna → important ritual practice.\nRig Veda is the principal source for understanding early Vedic religion."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which of the following statements about the Brahmo Samaj is correct?","A":"It promoted idol worship and strengthened the priestly class.","B":"It opposed idolatry and questioned the necessity of a priestly class.","C":"It rejected all forms of religious reform.","D":"It declared the Vedas to be infallible and compulsory for worship.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Brahmo Samaj","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Raja Rammohan Roy founded the Brahmo Sabha, later known as Brahmo Samaj.\nThe movement opposed idol worship.\nIt promoted monotheism and religious reform.\nIt challenged several social and religious practices and did not regard the Vedas as infallible in the manner stated.","revisionPoints":"Brahmo Samaj → associated with Raja Rammohan Roy.\nFounded in 1828.\nEmphasised monotheism.\nOpposed idolatry and promoted social and religious reform."},
{"no":75,"text":"The Reserve Bank of India acts as a bankers' bank because:\n1. Commercial banks maintain deposits with the RBI.\n2. RBI provides funds to commercial banks when required.\n3. RBI advises the government on monetary matters.\nWhich of the statements given above correctly explain the term?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["RBI","Bankers Bank","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Commercial banks maintain certain balances with the RBI.\nRBI provides liquidity and lending facilities to banks under its monetary framework.\nThese functions establish the RBI's role as the banker to banks.\nAdvising the government is an important RBI function, but it does not define the specific term \"bankers' bank.\"","revisionPoints":"RBI → central bank of India.\nBanker to banks → maintains bank reserves and provides liquidity.\nBanker to government → manages government banking functions.\nRBI also conducts monetary policy and regulates the banking system."},
{"no":76,"text":"Capital gains can arise from:\n1. Natural increase in the value of property\n2. Increase in the value of a painting due to its growing popularity\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Taxation","tags":["Capital Gains","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Capital gain generally arises when a capital asset increases in value and is transferred, subject to applicable tax rules.\nAppreciation in property can result in a capital gain upon transfer.\nA painting may also appreciate because of increased popularity or demand.\nThe taxable treatment depends on the nature of the asset and applicable tax provisions.","revisionPoints":"Capital gain → associated with transfer of capital assets.\nAsset appreciation alone is not necessarily the same as a realised capital gain.\nExamples can include property, shares and works of art.\nTax treatment depends on the applicable legal classification."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which of the following can increase the money supply in an economy?\n1. Central bank purchasing government securities from the public\n2. Government borrowing from the central bank\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Money Supply","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"When the central bank purchases government securities from the public, it injects money into the banking/financial system.\nGovernment borrowing directly from the central bank can also increase the monetary base.\nThese actions can increase liquidity and influence the overall money supply.\nThe actual effect depends on the monetary and banking conditions of the economy.","revisionPoints":"Central bank → controls monetary conditions through several instruments.\nPurchase of government securities → liquidity injection.\nGovernment borrowing from central bank can expand base money.\nMoney supply is influenced by both central bank actions and commercial bank credit creation."},
{"no":78,"text":"Foreign Direct Investment (FDI) includes which of the following?\n1. Subsidiaries of foreign companies operating in India\n2. Companies in which foreign investors hold a majority equity stake\n3. Companies exclusively financed by foreign companies\n4. Portfolio investment in Indian stock markets\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Foreign Direct Investment","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"FDI represents investment involving a lasting interest and significant influence/control in an enterprise.\nForeign-owned subsidiaries are examples of direct investment.\nMajority foreign equity ownership can constitute FDI.\nPortfolio investment involves financial securities without the same degree of direct control and is classified separately.","revisionPoints":"FDI → investment involving lasting interest in an enterprise.\nFPI → primarily investment in financial securities.\nFDI can bring capital, technology and management expertise.\nFDI policy is subject to sectoral and regulatory conditions."},
{"no":79,"text":"According to the source, the international price of a country's currency is primarily influenced by:\n1. Demand for its goods and services\n2. Stability of its government\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Currency Valuation","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Demand for a country's goods and services can influence demand for its currency through international transactions.\nPolitical and institutional stability can affect investor and market confidence.\nExchange rates, however, are influenced by several additional factors such as interest rates, inflation, capital flows and expectations.\nThe question identifies the two factors listed in the source.","revisionPoints":"Exchange rate → price of one currency in terms of another.\nInfluenced by demand and supply of currencies.\nInterest rates, inflation and capital flows can affect exchange rates.\nGovernment and institutional stability can influence market confidence."},
{"no":80,"text":"The Lead Bank Scheme was introduced with the objective of:","A":"Making one bank responsible for all banking activities in India","B":"Giving each State control over a particular commercial bank","C":"Assigning particular districts to individual banks for intensive development","D":"Providing only agricultural loans through cooperative banks","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Lead Bank Scheme","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Lead Bank Scheme was introduced to improve banking development in districts.\nEach district was assigned a particular bank as the lead bank.\nThe lead bank was expected to coordinate banking activities and improve financial access.\nThe scheme aimed particularly at extending banking facilities to underserved areas.","revisionPoints":"Lead Bank Scheme → introduced in 1969.\nFocus → district-level banking development.\nLead bank → coordinates banking activities in its assigned district.\nImportant for expanding financial inclusion and rural credit."},
{"no":81,"text":"Which of the following were not introduced into India by the English?\n1. Assessment of land revenue based on soil and crop productivity\n2. Mobile cannons\n3. Tobacco and red chillies\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["English Colonial Introductions","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Land revenue assessment based on soil and crop characteristics existed in India before British rule.\nMobile artillery/cannons were known in India before the arrival of the English.\nTobacco and red chillies had also reached India through earlier contacts and were not introduced by the English.","revisionPoints":"Do not assume every agricultural or military innovation during colonial times originated with the British.\nTobacco originated in the Americas.\nChilli peppers also originated in the Americas.\nIndian revenue systems existed in various forms before British administration."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements about ancient Indian guilds:\n1. Guilds fixed wages and rules for their members.\n2. Guilds could regulate standards and prices.\n3. Guilds possessed judicial powers over their members.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Guilds","Shreni","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Ancient guilds, or shrenis, organised people belonging to particular occupations.\nThey could establish rules governing members and regulate professional activities.\nGuilds could also exercise authority over disputes involving their members.\nTheir role extended beyond simple economic cooperation.","revisionPoints":"Guilds were known as Shrenis in ancient India.\nThey organised artisans, merchants and other occupational groups.\nFunctions included regulation of wages, standards and prices.\nThey could exercise internal disciplinary and judicial functions."},
{"no":83,"text":"The distribution of powers between the Centre and the States in India is substantially based on which of the following?","A":"Government of India Act, 1909","B":"Government of India Act, 1919","C":"Government of India Act, 1935","D":"Indian Councils Act, 1892","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Government of India Act 1935","Centre-State Relations","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Government of India Act, 1935 introduced a detailed framework for the distribution of legislative powers.\nIt provided for separate legislative lists.\nThe Constitution subsequently developed and adapted this federal structure.\nThe present Constitution distributes legislative powers through the Seventh Schedule.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1935 → major influence on India's constitutional structure.\nLegislative powers → Union, State and Concurrent Lists.\nPresent constitutional basis → Seventh Schedule.\nIndia combines federal features with a strong Union."},
{"no":84,"text":"Why does India import coal despite having substantial domestic coal reserves?\n1. Some power plants face inadequate domestic coal supply.\n2. Steel companies require specific grades of coking coal.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Coal Imports","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"India's domestic coal production does not always meet the quantity and quality requirements of all consumers.\nCertain power plants may require additional supplies.\nThe steel industry needs high-quality coking coal for steel production.\nTherefore, imports can occur even when substantial domestic reserves exist.","revisionPoints":"India has large coal reserves.\nCoking coal is particularly important for the iron and steel industry.\nCoal imports may occur due to quality, availability and logistical constraints.\nThermal power remains an important consumer of coal."},
{"no":85,"text":"A traveller wants to travel towards the east. If the Pole Star is kept on his right side, in which direction is he travelling?","A":"North","B":"South","C":"West","D":"East","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Pole Star","Navigation","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Pole Star indicates approximately the direction of north in the Northern Hemisphere.\nIf north is on your right, you are facing east.\nTherefore, keeping the Pole Star on the right side while travelling means moving eastward.","revisionPoints":"Pole Star → approximately indicates north.\nFacing north → east is on the right.\nFacing east → north is on the left.\nPole Star navigation is particularly useful in the Northern Hemisphere."},
{"no":86,"text":"Why are rare earth elements strategically important?\n1. China has imposed restrictions affecting their export.\n2. Demand for rare earth elements is increasing because they are important for modern electronics.\n3. Only China, Australia, Canada and Chile possess rare earth resources.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Mining & Minerals","tags":["Rare Earth Metals","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Rare earth elements are important inputs for several high-technology industries.\nThey are used in electronics, magnets, renewable-energy technologies and other advanced applications.\nChina has historically played a major role in global rare-earth supply chains.\nRare earth resources are not limited to only four countries.","revisionPoints":"Rare earth elements → group of 17 elements.\nImportant for electronics and advanced technologies.\nUsed in permanent magnets, electronics and clean-energy technologies.\nResource availability and processing capacity have major strategic significance."},
{"no":87,"text":"Which of the following are tiger reserves?\n1. Bandipur\n2. Bhitarkanika\n3. Manas\n4. Sundarbans\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Tiger Reserves","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Bandipur in Karnataka is a tiger reserve.\nManas in Assam is a tiger reserve.\nSundarbans in West Bengal is a tiger reserve.\nBhitarkanika in Odisha is a major mangrove and wildlife ecosystem, but it is not a tiger reserve.","revisionPoints":"Bandipur → Karnataka.\nManas → Assam.\nSundarbans → West Bengal.\nBhitarkanika → famous for mangroves and saltwater crocodiles."},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Indian monsoon:\n1. The duration of the monsoon generally decreases from south to north.\n2. Annual rainfall in the northern plains generally decreases from east to west.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Monsoon","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The monsoon reaches southern parts of India earlier and generally remains there for a longer period.\nAs one moves northward, the monsoon season becomes relatively shorter.\nThe northern plains receive progressively less rainfall from east towards west.\nThis is related to the movement and weakening of monsoon winds across the plains.","revisionPoints":"Southwest monsoon → major source of India's annual rainfall.\nMonsoon duration generally decreases south → north.\nRainfall in northern plains generally decreases east → west.\nMonsoon variability strongly affects agriculture and water resources."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of the Tropical Savanna climate?","A":"Rainfall throughout the year with no dry season","B":"Extremely cold winters and short summers","C":"Permanent snow cover","D":"Distinct wet and dry seasons","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Tropical Savannah","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Tropical Savanna regions have a generally warm climate throughout the year.\nRainfall is concentrated in a particular season.\nA pronounced dry season occurs during the rest of the year.\nNatural vegetation commonly includes grasslands with scattered trees.","revisionPoints":"Tropical Savanna → Aw climate in the Köppen classification.\nDistinct wet and dry seasons.\nCommon vegetation → tall grasses and scattered trees.\nFound in parts of Africa, South America, Australia and India."},
{"no":90,"text":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a National Park from areas where local communities traditionally collect biomass?","A":"Local people have unrestricted rights to collect biomass in National Parks.","B":"Local people are generally not allowed to collect or use biomass from National Parks.","C":"National Parks permit commercial extraction of forest resources.","D":"National Parks are created only for timber production.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Law","tags":["Protected Areas","National Parks","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"National Parks receive a high degree of legal protection.\nActivities such as resource extraction and grazing are generally highly restricted or prohibited.\nThis differs from some other protected-area categories where specified rights may continue subject to legal provisions.\nThe primary objective is ecosystem and wildlife conservation.","revisionPoints":"National Park → high level of legal protection.\nHuman activities are more restricted than in many other protected areas.\nWildlife conservation is a major objective.\nDistinguish National Parks from Wildlife Sanctuaries and Biosphere Reserves."},
{"no":91,"text":"Which of the following can act as pollinating agents?\n1. Bats\n2. Bees\n3. Birds\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Pollinators","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Bees are among the most important pollinating insects.\nSeveral species of birds transfer pollen while feeding on flowers.\nSome bats are important pollinators, especially in tropical ecosystems.\nPollination is therefore not restricted to insects.","revisionPoints":"Pollinators include insects, birds and mammals.\nBees are important pollinators of many agricultural crops.\nBats can pollinate certain tropical and nocturnal flowers.\nPollination is essential for sexual reproduction in flowering plants."},
{"no":92,"text":"Which of the following groups consists entirely of endangered species?","A":"Great Indian Bustard, Musk Deer, Red Panda, Asiatic Wild Ass","B":"Indian Elephant, Indian Peafowl, Cattle Egret, House Sparrow","C":"Common Myna, Rock Pigeon, House Crow, Indian Roller","D":"Blue Bull, Wild Boar, Rhesus Monkey, Golden Jackal","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Endangered Species","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The species listed in option (a) face significant conservation concerns.\nThe Great Indian Bustard is particularly threatened by habitat loss and infrastructure-related risks.\nRed Panda and musk deer also face pressures from habitat loss and other threats.\nThe Asiatic Wild Ass is associated with the arid and semi-arid landscapes of western India.","revisionPoints":"Great Indian Bustard → grassland ecosystem.\nRed Panda → eastern Himalayan forests.\nMusk Deer → Himalayan and high-altitude ecosystems.\nAsiatic Wild Ass → mainly associated with the Little Rann of Kutch."},
{"no":93,"text":"If capillary action did not occur, which of the following would be affected?\n1. Functioning of a kerosene lamp\n2. Absorption of ink by blotting paper\n3. Growth of large trees\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Capillarity","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Capillary action is the movement of liquid through narrow spaces due to adhesion and cohesion.\nKerosene can move through the narrow pores of a lamp wick through capillary action.\nInk spreads through the fibres of blotting paper by the same principle.\nPlants use narrow conducting tissues to transport water upward, with capillary forces contributing to water movement.","revisionPoints":"Capillarity → movement of liquid through narrow spaces.\nDepends on adhesion and cohesion.\nImportant in wicks and absorbent materials.\nPlays a role in water movement in plants along with transpiration pull and other forces."},
{"no":94,"text":"Which of the following are examples of supporting ecosystem services?\n1. Nutrient cycling\n2. Crop pollination\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Ecosystem Services","Millennium Ecosystem Assessment","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Supporting services are ecosystem processes that sustain the functioning of ecosystems.\nNutrient cycling is essential for maintaining soil fertility and ecosystem productivity.\nPollination supports plant reproduction and agricultural production.\nThese processes underpin several other ecosystem services.","revisionPoints":"Major ecosystem service categories include:\nProvisioning → food, water, timber.\nRegulating → climate regulation, flood control.\nCultural → recreation and spiritual values.\nSupporting → nutrient cycling and ecosystem productivity."},
{"no":95,"text":"Which of the following correctly distinguishes the ecological adaptations of Oryx and Chiru?","A":"Oryx is adapted to hot arid regions, while Chiru is adapted to cold high-altitude regions.","B":"Oryx is adapted to polar regions, while Chiru is adapted to tropical forests.","C":"Both are primarily adapted to tropical rainforests.","D":"Both are primarily adapted to coastal ecosystems.","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Oryx","Chiru","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Oryx is adapted to dry and hot environments where water is limited.\nChiru, or Tibetan antelope, is associated with the cold, high-altitude landscapes of the Tibetan Plateau.\nTheir contrasting habitats demonstrate adaptation to different temperature and moisture conditions.","revisionPoints":"Oryx → arid and semi-arid ecosystems.\nChiru → Tibetan Plateau/high-altitude steppes.\nChiru is associated with cold, dry environments.\nHabitat adaptation is reflected in animals' physiology and behaviour."},
{"no":96,"text":"Which of the following are major threats to biodiversity?\n1. Global warming\n2. Habitat fragmentation\n3. Invasion by alien species\n4. Vegetarianism\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Threats to Biodiversity","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Climate change/global warming can alter habitats and species' ecological ranges.\nHabitat fragmentation isolates populations and reduces habitat connectivity.\nInvasive alien species can compete with, prey upon or otherwise disrupt native species.\nVegetarianism is not classified as a major direct threat to biodiversity in this context.","revisionPoints":"Major biodiversity threats include:\nHabitat loss and fragmentation\nInvasive alien species\nClimate change\nOverexploitation and pollution\nHabitat fragmentation can reduce gene flow between populations."},
{"no":97,"text":"Which of the following species are naturally found in India?\n1. Black-necked Crane\n2. Flying Squirrel\n3. Snow Leopard\n4. Cheetah\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1, 2 and 3 only","C":"2, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Indian Fauna","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"The Black-necked Crane occurs in high-altitude wetlands, particularly in the Himalayan region.\nFlying squirrels occur in forest ecosystems in different parts of India.\nThe Snow Leopard inhabits high-altitude Himalayan and trans-Himalayan landscapes.\nThe source treats the cheetah as not naturally occurring in India.","revisionPoints":"Black-necked Crane → high-altitude wetland species.\nSnow Leopard → Himalayan high-altitude ecosystem.\nFlying squirrels → forest-dependent mammals.\nThe cheetah was historically present in India but was considered extinct in the wild in India before its recent reintroduction programme."},
{"no":98,"text":"Which of the following agricultural practices contribute to carbon sequestration/storage in soil?\n1. Contour bunding\n2. Relay cropping\n3. Zero tillage\nSelect the correct answer using the code below:","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Carbon Sequestration","Zero Tillage","UPSC","2012"],"expl":"Contour bunding reduces soil erosion and helps retain soil and organic matter.\nRelay cropping allows another crop to be established before the previous crop is harvested, improving land cover.\nZero tillage minimises soil disturbance and can help retain soil organic carbon.\nThese practices are associated with conservation agriculture and improved soil management.","revisionPoints":"Soil carbon sequestration → storage of carbon in soil organic matter.\nZero tillage → reduced soil disturbance.\nContour bunding → reduces runoff and soil erosion.\nMaintaining soil cover can improve soil health and carbon retention."},
];
